  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Methodology & data quality</h1>
    <p>
      This page documents how monthly occupancy reports are generated so reviewers can understand
      what the numbers represent—and where they should be treated as estimates.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="panel prose">
    <h2>Purpose</h2>
    <p>
      The University of Virginia Library maintains monthly visit and occupancy summaries for its
      public facilities. These reports support internal assessment and operational planning.
    </p>

    <h2>Data source</h2>
    <p>
      Raw counts come from entrance and portal cameras that track people moving into and out of each
      building. Counts are stored centrally and processed by the Library’s occupancy reporting
      service (MCP gateway), which:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Aggregates camera serials belonging to each library building</li>
      <li>Derives per-minute entries, exits, and estimated occupancy</li>
      <li>Aligns analysis windows to published LibCal open hours for that building</li>
      <li>Applies daily reset and off-hours suppression logic to reduce counter drift</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What “entries” means</h2>
    <p>
      <strong>Total entries</strong> (in traffic) is the best single figure for “how many visits
      occurred during open hours in this month.” Exits and combined activity provide additional
      operational context. Reports intentionally exclude activity outside open hours (with a short
      buffer around opening and closing).
    </p>

    <h2>Estimates — especially Shannon and Clemons</h2>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>Important:</strong> Counts for all buildings are estimates. Shannon and Clemons are
      particularly imperfect because of complex multi-entrance layouts, high concurrent traffic, and
      camera geometry. Even so, the current camera-based system is <em>more reliable and more
      complete</em> than the Library’s previous gate-counter approach, which frequently under-counted
      or missed side entrances entirely.
    </div>
    <p>
      Branch libraries (Fine Arts, Music, Science &amp; Engineering) generally have simpler entry
      patterns, but they remain estimates of true headcount as well.
    </p>

    <h2>Reporting window</h2>
    <p>
      The site publishes the last 24 complete calendar months for every library that appears in the
      occupancy database: Clemons, Fine Arts, Music, Science &amp; Engineering, and Shannon. Camera
      history in the production database begins around mid-October 2023; months before reliable
      coverage may show gaps or errors.
    </p>

    <h2>How reports are built for this site</h2>
    <p>
      Static pages are generated with <a href="https://astro.build/">Astro</a>. At data-refresh time,
      a build script calls the public occupancy MCP gateway:
    </p>
    <p>
      <code style="font-size:0.85rem;word-break:break-all">
        https://occupancy-reporting-gateway-mohw8c1jug.gateway.bedrock-agentcore.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/mcp
      </code>
    </p>
    <p>
      using the <code>get_occupancy_report</code> tool for each library and month. Parsed metrics
      power summary tables; the full executive report text is retained on each monthly page for
      archival use.
    </p>

    <h2>Recommended citation</h2>
    <p>A typical citation might read:</p>
    <p>
      <em
        >University of Virginia Library. Monthly Occupancy Report: [Library], [Month Year]. Derived
        from entrance camera counts during published open hours. Figures are estimates of facility
        use.</em
      >
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
